 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "sexum-mutavit",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Bold Diction",
   "quote": "sexum audacia mutavit",
   "trans": "she changed her sex by her daring",
   "body": "A startling phrase: Psyche, helped by Fate, \"changes her sex\" — she finds a man’s courage. <em>audacia</em> (\"daring\") is the means; the verb <em>mutavit</em> makes the transformation sound almost physical, fitting a book of <em>Metamorphoses</em>.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The bold phrase <em>sexum audacia mutavit</em> casts Psyche’s sudden courage as a change of sex, an arresting idea in a work about transformation; <em>audacia</em> stresses that it is daring, not nature, that steels her.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "mitissimam-bestiam",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "omnium ferarum mitissimam dulcissimamque bestiam",
   "trans": "the gentlest and sweetest beast of all wild animals",
   "body": "The \"monster\" her sisters described is redefined with two superlatives, <em>mitissimam</em> and <em>dulcissimam</em>. To call a god the gentlest and sweetest <em>bestia</em> of all wild animals is pointed irony — the dreaded beast is Love himself.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The superlatives <em>mitissimam dulcissimamque</em> applied to a <em>bestia</em> create irony: the terrifying monster of the sisters’ tale is in fact the gentlest, sweetest creature alive — the god Cupid.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "formonsum-formonse",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Polyptoton",
   "quote": "formonsum deum formonse cubantem",
   "trans": "a handsome god reclining handsomely",
   "body": "The adjective <em>formonsum</em> and the adverb <em>formonse</em> frame <em>deum</em> in a play on the same root (<em>polyptoton</em>). The repeated sound lingers admiringly on Cupid’s beauty — handsome, and handsomely posed.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The polyptoton <em>formonsum ... formonse</em> rings a change on the same root to dwell on Cupid’s beauty: he is not only a handsome god but lies there handsomely, the repetition mirroring Psyche’s lingering gaze.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "lumen-increbruit",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Personification",
   "quote": "lucernae quoque lumen hilaratum increbruit",
   "trans": "even the light of the lamp grew brighter, cheered",
   "body": "The lamp’s flame is personified: it is <em>hilaratum</em> (\"gladdened\") and flares up at the sight of the god. The inanimate light shares in the wonder of the scene.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"Personification animates the scene: <em>lumen hilaratum increbruit</em> makes the lamp itself rejoice and burn brighter at the sight of Cupid, as if nature delights in the god of love.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
